Step 8 — Authentication and Security

Inspect and preserve the existing authentication model.

Determine whether the RT agent requires:

* bearer tokens
* session cookies
* API keys
* mutual TLS
* signed requests
* service-to-service authentication
* CSRF protection

Implement authentication using the existing UI and backend patterns.

Do not expose secrets in client-side code.

Do not log:

* access tokens
* API keys
* sensitive user data
* private agent responses
* confidential request metadata

Preserve existing sanitization and security rules.

⸻

Step 9 — CORS, Proxy, and Network Configuration

Verify how the UI is expected to reach the RT agent.

Determine whether the integration requires:

* direct browser-to-agent access
* a UI backend proxy
* a reverse proxy
* API gateway routing
* CORS configuration
* WebSocket configuration
* Server-Sent Events configuration

If direct browser access is intended, update the RT agent CORS configuration only according to existing project conventions.

If a proxy is required, implement the proxy using the existing UI or platform architecture.

Do not introduce an unnecessary network layer.

